[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97051"] :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- $A+2I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "06ef2ccc-b41d-5292-8290-0acb5f6567e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "79294574-7441-5453-865a-e124567cf0e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6c2daa22-1727-5a47-850e-1f9e36cb83d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f27da12d-2633-5cc5-ad9f-cb1d359e064c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "650d3df9-0dfc-5d9d-95d6-53e957a7bad7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c78716-464b-5ab8-bd64-0dcbe92d55db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97897/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-97897/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-97897/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-97897/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-97897/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-97897/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97897" :question/id #uuid "7bae13f0-ed61-5405-af6c-4709e99174f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97897" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97897/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97897"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "add9c991-f8b2-51b5-895e-990ca0db9f35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5a84ce37-8f0a-5827-939f-9a4330caa90b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "79ba487f-28c5-5d1b-9e56-d271d9ce9e3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "720e8604-6c59-5755-887f-9bc351c6a1f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "181ff9b1-dc77-57c8-8058-5983ffd433d9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6975df52-b21a-5a04-958e-e8353d9cf26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97948/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-97948/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-97948/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-97948/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-97948/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-97948/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97948" :question/id #uuid "5aa9b75a-073a-5562-97b5-f48211bd8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-97948" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C-5I$ is a non-singular matrix.\n- $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3$\n- $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97948/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97948"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97961"] :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f786244b-d7da-5cd5-a693-cd6a8506d190" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "71a86466-ffa3-5b77-9b27-b82a6408f55b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "dd5bb9d6-bfd5-58a8-8976-f6f11a7fad53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "1820f85e-ec5f-5411-8cd0-ce8cffc2d20b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d7919dd8-ed38-52f0-a754-938b9dcd75ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0885b831-dde7-5596-ad72-d6e1b784a642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97969/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-97969/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-97969/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-97969/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-97969/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-97969/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97969" :question/id #uuid "c08b449a-000e-584f-a553-bf350d70d374" :question/math-academy-id "q-97969" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- $A-3I$ is invertible\n- $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97969/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97969"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
